WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:07.460
Two kinds of sensory axon enter the spinal cord side by side.

2
00:00:08.260 --> 00:00:10.160
One kind stops in the dorsal horn.

3
00:00:11.020 --> 00:00:12.780
The other runs on to the motor neurons.

4
00:00:13.700 --> 00:00:14.700
What sorts them?

5
00:00:16.183 --> 00:00:19.463
The ones that stop are small pain and temperature fibres.

6
00:00:20.443 --> 00:00:25.263
They depend on nerve growth factor The ones that go on are large muscle stretch

7
00:00:25.263 --> 00:00:28.343
fibres. They depend on neurotrophin-3 (NT-3).

8
00:00:29.917 --> 00:00:34.797
The ventral cord was known to release something that inhibits sensory axon growth.

9
00:00:35.557 --> 00:00:37.457
A map needs walls as well as roads.

10
00:00:38.650 --> 00:00:40.590
To find a wall, you need an assay.

11
00:00:41.630 --> 00:00:44.770
Raper and Kapfhammer grew chick sensory neurons in a dish.

12
00:00:45.630 --> 00:00:48.030
Then they added membranes from embryonic brain.

13
00:00:49.683 --> 00:00:53.703
Within minutes, many of the spread growth cones folded into thin tips.

14
00:00:54.503 --> 00:00:58.163
That is collapse. The fraction that collapsed was the readout.

15
00:00:59.003 --> 00:01:01.523
Remove the membranes, and the effect reverses.

16
00:01:03.467 --> 00:01:04.947
Would any membrane do this?

17
00:01:05.707 --> 00:01:07.347
And what kind of molecule is doing it?

18
00:01:13.250 --> 00:01:16.790
Membranes from liver or fibroblasts did not cause collapse.

19
00:01:17.670 --> 00:01:19.270
So not just any membrane would do.

20
00:01:19.990 --> 00:01:22.130
Heat and trypsin destroyed the activity.

21
00:01:22.970 --> 00:01:23.970
So it was a protein.

22
00:01:25.883 --> 00:01:29.763
Raper's group purified it from chick brain and named it collapsin.

23
00:01:30.803 --> 00:01:33.203
Today it is called Sema3A, a semaphorin.

24
00:01:34.933 --> 00:01:36.293
Then came a first clue.

25
00:01:36.293 --> 00:01:39.893
Growth cones from the retina did not respond to it.

26
00:01:42.967 --> 00:01:44.847
Here is the idea to carry through.

27
00:01:45.707 --> 00:01:46.727
The cue marks the place.

28
00:01:47.567 --> 00:01:49.367
The growth cone decides what it means.

29
00:01:55.967 --> 00:01:58.107
Does a growth cone answer the cue at all?

30
00:01:58.987 --> 00:02:00.267
First, it needs the receptor.

31
00:02:01.950 --> 00:02:03.310
Which way does it answer?

32
00:02:04.170 --> 00:02:05.790
That depends on its internal state.

33
00:02:06.883 --> 00:02:09.343
If it is repelled, does it turn, or collapse?

34
00:02:10.283 --> 00:02:13.723
In typical assays, a gradient from one side turns it away.

35
00:02:14.563 --> 00:02:18.643
A high dose all round can fold it up, where the preparation supports collapse.

36
00:02:19.663 --> 00:02:22.163
Layout, dose, exposure and the cell all matter.

37
00:02:23.917 --> 00:02:25.197
The rule has limits.

38
00:02:25.977 --> 00:02:28.617
Which cue is made, where, and how much still matter.

39
00:02:29.477 --> 00:02:32.057
And in the living cord, other cues share the job.

40
00:02:33.300 --> 00:02:34.800
Back to the two axons.

41
00:02:35.640 --> 00:02:36.760
One wall, two answers.

42
00:02:37.620 --> 00:02:41.800
The takeaway: the same cue stops one axon, and barely affects the other.
